{"name":"Kashmir Blue","hex":"#576d8e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kashmir","blocks":[{"text":"Kashmir is the northernmost geographical region of the Indian subcontinent. Until the mid-19th century, the term Kashmir denoted only the Kashmir Valley between the Great Himalayas and the Pir Panjal Range. The term has since also come to encompass a larger area that formerly comprised the princely state of Jammu and Kashmir, and includes the Indian-administered territories of Jammu and Kashmir and Ladakh, the Pakistani-administered territories of Azad Kashmir and Gilgit-Baltistan, and the Chinese-administered territories of Aksai Chin and the Trans-Karakoram Tract."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Kashmir_region._LOC_2003626427_-_showing_sub-regions_administered_by_different_countries.jpg/330px-Kashmir_region._LOC_2003626427_-_showing_sub-regions_administered_by_different_countries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kashmir","wikidata":"Q43100"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Maharajah_Gulab_Singh_%281792-1857%29_seated_holding_a_sword_against_a_bolster_on_a_terrace.webp/500px-Maharajah_Gulab_Singh_%281792-1857%29_seated_holding_a_sword_against_a_bolster_on_a_terrace.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Maharajah_Gulab_Singh_%281792-1857%29_seated_holding_a_sword_against_a_bolster_on_a_terrace.webp/1280px-Maharajah_Gulab_Singh_%281792-1857%29_seated_holding_a_sword_against_a_bolster_on_a_terrace.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Maharajah_Gulab_Singh_%281792-1857%29_seated_holding_a_sword_against_a_bolster_on_a_terrace.webp/500px-Maharajah_Gulab_Singh_%281792-1857%29_seated_holding_a_sword_against_a_bolster_on_a_terrace.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gulab Singh, the first Maharaja of the princely state of Jammu and Kashmir","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaharajah_Gulab_Singh_(1792-1857)_seated_holding_a_sword_against_a_bolster_on_a_terrace.webp"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/08/NWFP-Kashmir1909-a.jpg/500px-NWFP-Kashmir1909-a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/08/NWFP-Kashmir1909-a.jpg/500px-NW